
class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {

        // prefix stores the product of elements
        // when traversing the array from left to right
        int prefix = 1;

        // suffix stores the product of elements
        // when traversing the array from right to left
        int suffix = 1;

        // ans keeps track of the maximum product found so far
        // initialized to the smallest integer to handle
        // cases where all numbers are negative
        int ans = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        // length of the array
        int n = nums.length;

        // single loop to traverse the array from both ends
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            /*
             * If prefix becomes 0, it means we encountered a zero earlier.
             * Any product multiplied by 0 becomes 0.
             * So we reset prefix to 1 to start a new subarray
             * after the zero.
             */
            if (prefix == 0) {
                prefix = 1;
            }

            /*
             * Same logic applies to suffix.
             * If suffix becomes 0, reset it to 1 so that
             * we can start calculating product from the right
             * side again after a zero.
             */
            if (suffix == 0) {
                suffix = 1;
            }

            /*
             * Multiply the current element while moving from left.
             * This considers subarrays ending at index i.
             */
            prefix = prefix * nums[i];

            /*
             * Multiply the current element while moving from right.
             * This considers subarrays starting at index (n - i - 1).
             */
            suffix = suffix * nums[n - i - 1];

            /*
             * The maximum product subarray could be found
             * either from the left scan (prefix)
             * or from the right scan (suffix).
             * So we take the maximum of:
             *  - current ans
             *  - current prefix
             *  - current suffix
             */
            ans = Math.max(ans, Math.max(prefix, suffix));
        }

        // return the maximum product subarray found
        return ans;
    }

}


🧠 Why this algorithm works (intuition)

A zero breaks any product → reset

A negative number flips the sign

Maximum product may:

start after a zero

end before a zero

Scanning both directions ensures we don’t miss valid subarrays

⏱ Complexity

Time: O(n)

Space: O(1)
